**BUG-104: Fixed-amount coupons split the discount strangely across lines**
"With a €10 voucher on an order of three equally priced lines, the first line got a much bigger discount than the others. Finance says the split must follow SPEC 2.3."

The `allocate` function in money.js is:
```js
function allocate(totalCents, weights) {
  const sum = weights.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  if (sum === 0) return weights.map(() => 0);
  const parts = weights.map((w) => Math.floor((totalCents * w) / sum));
  let remainder = totalCents - parts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  parts[0] += remainder;
  return parts;
}
```

This only adds the remainder to `parts[0]` (the first element). That's wrong — per SPEC 2.3, ties are broken in favor of the earlier part. The largest remainder method requires distributing the remainder one cent at a time to the parts with the largest remainders, and ties go to the earlier part.

The current code always adds the entire remainder to `parts[0]`. For example, `allocate(10, [3, 3, 4])`: weights sum = 10. parts = floor(10*3/10)=3, floor(10*3/10)=3, floor(10*4/10)=4 → [3,3,4], remainder 0. OK.

For `allocate(7, [1, 2, 2, 1])`: sum=6. parts = [1,2,2,1] (sum 6), remainder 1. Current code: parts[0] += 1 → [2,2,2,1]. But the spec says the result should be [1,3,2,1] (tie between index 1 and 2 goes to earlier one). So the current code is wrong — it gives [2,2,2,1] instead of [1,3,2,1].

Fix: implement the largest remainder method properly:
```js
function allocate(totalCents, weights) {
  const sum = weights.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  if (sum === 0) return weights.map(() => 0);
  const parts = weights.map((w) => Math.floor((totalCents * w) / sum));
  let remainder = totalCents - parts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  // Distribute remainder to largest remainders, ties to earlier
  for (let i = 0; i < weights.length && remainder > 0; i++) {
    const idx = parts.indexOf(remainder); // no wait
  }
}
```

Better approach: create an array of {index, remainder} and sort by remainder descending, then by index ascending for ties. Or simpler:

```js
function allocate(totalCents, weights) {
  const sum = weights.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  if (sum === 0) return weights.map(() => 0);
  const parts = weights.map((w) => Math.floor((totalCents * w) / sum));
  const remainder = totalCents - parts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  // Distribute one cent at a time to largest remainders, ties to earlier
  for (let i = 0; i < weights.length && remainder > 0; i++) {
    // find the part with the largest remainder
    let bestIdx = 0;
    for (let j = 1; j < weights.length; j++) {
      const remJ = (totalCents * weights[j]) / sum - Math.floor((totalCents * weights[j]) / sum);
      const remI = (totalCents * weights[bestIdx]) / sum - Math.floor((totalCents * weights[bestIdx]) / sum);
      if (remJ > remI || (remJ === remI && j < bestIdx)) {
        bestIdx = j;
      }
    }
    parts[bestIdx]++;
    remainder--;
  }
  return parts;
}
```

Hmm, this is O(n²) per allocation but fine for small n. Actually, a cleaner way: compute the fractional part of each weight's share:

```js
function allocate(totalCents, weights) {
  const sum = weights.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  if (sum === 0) return weights.map(() => 0);
  const parts = weights.map((w) => Math.floor((totalCents * w) / sum));
  const remainder = totalCents - parts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  // Build list of (index, fractional_part) for all parts
  const fractional = weights.map((w, i) => ((totalCents * w) / sum - Math.floor((totalCents * w) / sum)));
  // Sort by fractional part descending, then index ascending
  const order = [...fractional.map((f, i) => [f, i])].sort((a, b) => {
    if (b[0] !== a[0]) return b[0] - a[0];
    return a[1] - b[1];
  });
  for (let k = 0; k < remainder && k < order.length; k++) {
    parts[order[k][1]]++;
  }
  return parts;
}
```

This is clean and correct.

